The medical school of Athens (and later, the medical schools of other cities) was founded in the 6th century BCE by Hippocratic physician Apollonius of Tyana, who had been brought to Athens from his native country of Lycia by the Athenians. Hippocras was one of the most famous physicians in history, and his medical writings are still widely used today. In the years leading up to the Greek Civil War, he was considered by many to be the greatest healer in all of Greece. His writings were so important to many Greek intellectuals that some of them were able to get a medical degree from the University of Leontini, on the island of Santorini. The medical education at León was very extensive, so it is no wonder that they could produce the doctors who were responsible for the victory of Sparta over the invading Persian Empire. When the Spartans were defeated by Athens in 404 BCE, it was Hippotas who led them back to Sparte. During the Peloponnesian War (431-404 BCE), the Spartan leader, Leonidas, was captured and killed by his fellow Spartans. He was buried in Spartaeum, where he is commemorated as one the Seven Sages of Olympus. This was a huge loss for Greece, because the city of Corinth had already been founded a hundred years earlier by a Spartan named Pericles. However, by this time, Hippopotas had become a famous hero of Greek mythology, with his death inspiring many other heroes, including the later Spartan hero, Achilles. It was in this period that the Hippo-thesian school was born.
